menu "TRMNL firmware"

choice TRMNL_WAIT_MODE
    prompt "What the device does between cycles"
    default TRMNL_WAIT_DEEP_SLEEP
    help
      Two builds of the same firmware, so this board's sleep current can be
      measured by flashing one and then the other.

      They are not equivalent: deep sleep costs the button. See the help on
      each option.

config TRMNL_WAIT_DEEP_SLEEP
    bool "Deep sleep, timer wake only"
    help
      esp_deep_sleep_start() with a timer wake-up, as upstream does. The SD
      card is unmounted and unpowered, Wi-Fi is torn down and the TPS65185 is
      put to sleep first. Each cycle is then a fresh boot, so expect about
      two seconds of start-up before the request goes out.

      KNOWN BAD ON ESP32-P4 CHIP REVISION 1.0/1.3: these silicon revisions
      have documented deep-sleep issues, fixed from revision v3.0 onward. If
      that's the chip on your board, use "Idle, button works" below instead -
      this is a hardware erratum to wait out on a fixed-revision board, not
      something this firmware can fix around. Check the chip revision with
      `idf.py -p PORT monitor` at boot, or `esptool.py chip_id`.

      THE BUTTON DOES NOT WORK IN THIS MODE. The P4 has 16 RTC-capable GPIOs
      (0-15) and the button is on GPIO35, so it cannot drive an ext0/ext1
      wake. Nothing polls it while the CPU is off; a press does nothing until
      the timer fires. Press RESET for an immediate refresh instead.

      Cross-cycle state (retry counters, the "needs a clean" flag, the last
      wake time) lives in RTC fast memory, which survives the sleep. The
      refresh interval and the device credentials are in NVS and survive
      anything.

config TRMNL_WAIT_IDLE
    bool "Idle, button works"
    help
      vTaskDelay() in 100 ms steps with the radio associated and the CPU
      clocked, polling the button so a press starts a cycle immediately.

      This is not a power-saving mode - it is the reference to measure deep
      sleep against, and the mode to use on a bench supply when the button
      matters more than the battery.

endchoice

choice TRMNL_PANEL
    prompt "Which e-paper panel is physically connected"
    default TRMNL_PANEL_ED103TC2
    help
      trmnl.app only renders content for panel resolutions it knows about,
      reported via the Model/Width/Height headers - see TRMNL_DISPLAY_WIDTH/
      HEIGHT in trmnl_config.h, which do NOT change with this choice. Picking
      a panel the server doesn't support here changes what this firmware
      does with what arrives, not what the server sends: the image is still
      decoded at the reported resolution, then scaled to fit whatever panel
      is actually connected via the same epd_fb_blit_fit_rot()/
      epd_fb_blit_1bpp_fit() path that already handles any size mismatch
      (trmnl_image.c, trmnl_bmp_render()) - there is no separate "scaling
      mode" to turn on, this just puts a differently-sized framebuffer in
      front of it.

config TRMNL_PANEL_ED103TC2
    bool "ED103TC2 - 10.3\", 1872x1404 (trmnl.app's own panel)"
    help
      What P0.0 and P1.2 verified against. Native resolution matches exactly
      what the server renders, so the fit path never actually triggers on
      real server content - only on locally-sized things like a status
      screen, which is already sized to the framebuffer either way.

config TRMNL_PANEL_ED133UT2
    bool "ED133UT2 - 13.3\", 2200x1650 (bench test - trmnl.app doesn't render for this size)"
    help
      Every server-sent frame is smaller than this panel in both dimensions
      (1872x1404 into 2200x1650), so every real cycle takes the upscale path:
      letterboxed, aspect preserved, pixel-repeat rather than interpolated
      (see epd_fb_blit_fit_rot()'s own doc comment on upscale quality).
      Watch the log for "fitting" - trmnl_image.c logs it whenever a decode
      doesn't match the framebuffer 1:1.

endchoice

config TRMNL_LOG_TEST_ON_BOOT
    bool "Send a test /api/log entry every cycle (debug)"
    default n
    help
      submit_log() in main.c only fires from a real failure
      (report_failure()'s call sites), which is awkward to trigger on demand
      without either breaking a working device or waiting for the real
      server to misbehave. This sends one harmless test entry to the real
      /api/log endpoint right after Wi-Fi and registration succeed each
      cycle, so the whole path - serialization, headers, POST, the server's
      actual response - can be checked against a real account without
      engineering a fake outage.

      Off by default and meant to be turned back off once you've confirmed
      it: this is a debug aid, not a feature, and it POSTs to the real
      server every single cycle for as long as it's enabled.

endmenu
